</asy>
</center>

In order to find the length of the elastic band, perpendicular lines are added to the figure to divide the band into sections for easier calculation. After the additions, the length of band consist of five identical lines and a full circle, the four shaded areas. The total length is five lines, the diameter of the coins, plus the circumference of a coin, so \(5d + 4\pi\). Finally, the length of the elastic band is <imath>\boxed{\textbf{(C)}4\pi+20}.</imath>
~TutorJack

==Solution 2 (Pure Geometry)==

As shown in the figure above, we just connect some lines and do relations to find the straight parts of the bands (That's easy). Now, we need to find the four curves. The three central circles form a triangle with angle measures <imath> 60^\circ </imath>. Extend the lines of that triangle and draw two perpendicular lines going through the top left circle's center. Then, it becomes obvious the angle measure for the two topmost arcs of the band is <imath> 60^\circ </imath>. Thus, the length of the curve is <imath> \frac{1}{6} \cdot \pi \cdot 2 \cdot 2 = \frac{2}{3}\pi </imath>. There are two of these, giving us a total of <imath> \frac{4}{3}\pi </imath>. Now, the bottom two. We do the same process and this time find out the measure of the arcs is <imath> 120^\circ </imath>, meaning the length of the curve is <imath> \frac{1}{3} \cdot 2 \cdot \pi \cdot 2 = \frac{4}{3} \pi </imath>. There are two such curves, and thus the total length is <imath> \frac{8}{3} \pi </imath>. The total curvature length is <imath> \frac{12}{3} \pi = 4\pi </imath>. The straight parts is just additive to <imath> 4+8+4+4 = 20 </imath>. Thus, the length of the band is <imath> \boxed{20+4\pi} </imath>.

~Pinotation

==Solution 3 (Using the answer choices, quickest and best for competitions)==

If you look at the answer choices you will notice that all the constants are the same, meaning that we just have to find the coefficient of pi. To do this we can form an isosceles trapezoid with the centers of the four corner circles.

<center>
<asy>
    unitsize(5mm);
    draw(circle((2,2),2));
    draw(circle((6,2),2));
    draw(circle((10,2),2));
    draw(circle((4,2+sqrt(12)),2));
    draw(circle((8,2+sqrt(12)),2));
    draw(arc((2,2),2,270,150) -- arc((4,2+sqrt(12)),2,150,90) .. arc((8,2+sqrt(12)),2,90,30) -- arc((10,2),2,30,-90) .. cycle, black+3bp);

    fill((2,2) -- arc((2,2),2,270,150) -- cycle, lightgray+opacity(0.4));
    fill((4,2+sqrt(12)) -- arc((4,2+sqrt(12)),2,150,90) -- cycle, lightgray+opacity(0.4));
    fill((8,2+sqrt(12)) -- arc((8,2+sqrt(12)),2,90,30) -- cycle, lightgray+opacity(0.4));
    fill((10,2) -- arc((10,2),2,30,-90) -- cycle, lightgray+opacity(0.4));

    draw((2,2) -- (4,2+sqrt(12)) -- (8,2+sqrt(12)) -- (10,2) -- (6,2) -- cycle);
    draw((2,2) -- arc((2,2),2,270,150) -- cycle);
    draw((4,2+sqrt(12)) -- arc((4,2+sqrt(12)),2,150,90) -- cycle);
    draw((8,2+sqrt(12)) -- arc((8,2+sqrt(12)),2,90,30) -- cycle);
    draw((10,2) -- arc((10,2),2,30,-90) -- cycle);
    draw((6,2) -- (6,0));

</asy>
</center>


Once we do this we can split the trapezoid into three equilateral triangles, after doing this we can solve for the curved lengths. The bottom 2 curved lengths are each <imath> 120^\circ </imath> as <imath>360-90-90-60=120,</imath> because this is an isosceles trapezoid the top two angles must be the same, thus they are both <imath> 60^\circ </imath> as <imath>\frac{360-(120+120)}{2}=60</imath> (We can also solve for this like we did for the bottom two by doing <imath>360-90-90-60-60=60</imath>) <imath>60+60+120+120=360</imath> so the total curved part is the same as the circumference of the circle which is diameter times <imath>pi</imath> which is 4*pi which = <imath>4\pi,</imath> so the answer is <imath>\boxed{\textbf{(C)}4\pi+20}.</imath>.

PS. For problems like these where a band is wrapped around congruent circles, the curved part is always equal to the circumference of a circle. 

~SShreyas

==Solution 5 (better visualization)==

[[File:2026 amc 8 q23 solution5.png | 600px]]

There are <imath>4</imath> fourths of a circle with radius <imath>2</imath>, so there is one circle with radius <imath>2</imath>. Finding the circumference gives <imath>4\pi</imath>, and adding the <imath>4</imath>'s created by matching the centers of one circle to another by using the radius property gives (it's always equal if it comes from the center) <imath>\boxed{\textbf{(C)}4\pi+20}.</imath>

-N.

==Solution 6 (Cheese🧀🧀🧀🧀🧀)==

We know that the coefficient of <imath>\pi</imath> must be <imath>2 \times 2</imath> because the angles in the trapezoid add to <imath>360^\circ</imath>, the circles are the same size, and the formula for the circumference of a circle is <imath>\pi d</imath>. The straight bits add to the rational bit and the curved bits add to the irrationals (with <imath>\pi</imath>). Therefore, the coefficient is <imath>4\pi</imath> so the correct answer is <imath>\boxed{\textbf{(C)}4\pi+20}.</imath>

~jeremyq 🧀

